{"availability":"available","full":"lokaz-c/erp-migration-demo","name":"erp-migration-demo","description":"Messy Excel books into a normalized PostgreSQL schema on synthetic data, with a scored data-quality report","category":"Other","language":"Python","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2026-10-05T22:56:49Z","updated":"2026-10-06T09:09:22Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://lokaz-c.github.io/erp-migration-demo/","topics":["data-migration","data-quality","etl","postgresql","python","sql"],"demo":"https://lokaz-c.github.io/erp-migration-demo/","color":"#63362a","ink":"#f8c7a8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-08T18:25:29.589Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-08T18:25:29.589Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-08T18:25:29.589Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-08T18:25:29.589Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Synthetic data, reconstructed approach. This repository reconstructs, on generated data, the approach used to migrate a fashion manufacturer's Excel books into a normalized SQL database. It is not DIKAM Fashion's production code or data. Every supplier, product, employee, salary and amount here is synthetic (names come from Faker with a fixed seed), and every number below was produced by running the code.","A seeded generator writes ten years of deliberately messy Excel books (purchases, sales, stock movements, payroll). A Python and SQL pipeline loads them into PostgreSQL 18, and a report shows what happened to every row: loaded, merged as a duplicate, rejected with a reason, or skipped as layout noise. The generator also writes the ground truth, so the pipeline is scored rather than eyeballed.","Why: migrating hand-kept books is mostly reconciliation. The same supplier is spelt ten ways, dates are typed day-first by one clerk and month-first by the next, amounts arrive in two currencies with the currency sometimes only inside the text (\"125,000 Frw\", \"$1,250.00\"), and rows are keyed twice across books. This repo shows how each of those was handled, with tests and measured results."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/lokaz-c/erp-migration-demo/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"ab3358ceb2224e4037f0232c006217079aca6c0726d49bee3acb8e580dedd293","extractedAt":"2026-10-08T18:25:29.716Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-08T18:25:29.716Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Synthetic data, reconstructed approach. This repository reconstructs, on generated data, the approach used to migrate a fashion manufacturer's Excel books into a normalized SQL database. It is not DIKAM Fashion's production code or data. Every supplier, product, employee, salary and amount here is synthetic (names come from Faker with a fixed seed), and every number below was produced by running the code.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/lokaz-c/erp-migration-demo/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[],"fingerprint":"ab3358ceb2224e4037f0232c006217079aca6c0726d49bee3acb8e580dedd293","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/lokaz-c/erp-migration-demo/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-08T18:25:29.716Z"},"overviewRepoUpdated":"2026-10-06T09:09:22Z","readmeSnapshot":{"version":1,"status":"saved","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/lokaz-c/erp-migration-demo/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-08T18:25:29.717Z","hash":"ab3358ceb2224e4037f0232c006217079aca6c0726d49bee3acb8e580dedd293","originalBytes":15541,"storedBytes":15541,"truncated":false,"searchTerms":["rows","text","books","bigint","report","row","truth","currency","numeric","date","supplier","sql","amount","docs","ground","make","purchase","against","data","every","load","month","one","product","core","dates","generator","key","match","merged","payroll","rates","two","check","code","employee","header","migration","order","orders","rejected","sheet","suppliers","unit","each","erp","gross","index.html","lines","movement","names","normalized","per","pipeline","read","rwf","sales","same","synthetic","amounts","